## The release gate (system-agnostic — keep)

Nothing ships until all clear: **provenance** (real non-zero build output from the intended commit, with
the expected routes) · **secrets contained** (no server-only value in the client bundle) · **version**
(immutable deployment id + commit recorded before promotion, so a rollback target exists) ·
**declarations honest** (privacy/cookie/analytics/disclosure match the built app + the live pages) ·
**promotion confirmed** (re-read {{RELEASE_CONFIRM}}, not the deploy response).

## Verification discipline (system-agnostic — keep — earned the hard way)

- 🔴 **Verify by artifact, never by a piped `| tail`** — the exit code is tail's; a failed build reports
  success. Redirect full output, grep it, confirm a real non-zero build output containing the routes you
  expect.
- 🔴 **Prove a check can find what you KNOW is there before trusting an all-clear** — a green check on the
  wrong signal is worse than none. Before trusting a "no secrets in the bundle" grep, plant a known string
  and confirm the grep finds it; minifiers rename identifiers, so searching for `MY_SECRET_KEY` can come
  back clean while its value sits in the bundle. **Grep for values, not names.**
- **Re-read the live URL to confirm a promotion** — fetch production and check for something only the new
  build has (version marker, changed route, new asset hash). An alias can still point at the previous
  deployment while the deploy command reports success.
- **Declarations are the fact in the shipped app, not the intention in config** — a disabled analytics
  flag is an intention; a network request from the live page is the fact. Read the running site.
- **Know the rollback before you need it** — record the previous deployment id first.

## Secrets (system-agnostic — keep)

Production secrets live in the host's environment store, never in git. Anything that must reach the
browser is public by definition — treat the client/server boundary as the security boundary, and verify
it in the built bundle rather than trusting the variable's prefix.

